## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference — a new reference on every render means a "changed" prop even if the data is identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object/function reference each time
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent renders |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation, or object/array passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function passed to a memoized child or used in a dep array |

**Key rule:** `useMemo`/`useCallback` are only useful when the consumer actually checks referential stability (e.g., a `React.memo` child, or a `useEffect` dep array). Memoizing a value that goes to a non-memoized child accomplishes nothing.

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every component that calls `useContext(MyContext)` re-renders when the context value changes. The trap: passing an inline object as value creates a new reference every render.

```jsx
// Bad — new object reference on every render, all consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}

// Good — stable reference, only re-renders when user actually changes
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return <UserContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</UserContext.Provider>;
}
```

For large contexts, split into separate contexts (e.g., `UserContext` + `UserDispatchContext`) so components only subscribe to what they need.

### 4. Before/After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` renders, even when `items` hasn't changed:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // new array reference each render

  const handleSelect = (item) => { // new function reference each render
    console.log(item);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* renders */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually change:

```jsx
const ITEMS = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // stable reference, defined outside component

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => {
    console.log(item);
  }, []); // stable reference — no deps that change

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={ITEMS} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* only re-renders when props change by reference */
});
```

**The fix required three things together:** `React.memo` on the child (opt into shallow comparison), `useCallback` for the function prop (stable reference), and a stable array (moved outside the component). Any one piece missing and the optimization doesn't work.

---

**Practical advice:** don't optimize preemptively. Profile with React DevTools Profiler first to confirm a component is actually slow before adding memoization — the overhead of stale closure bugs and cognitive complexity often outweighs the benefit for fast-rendering components.
